WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.159
Before we dive into the course, I want to explain a few things so you get the most out of this course.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.279
Take some time to listen to these. They might save you some headache.

3
00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:13.520
First, I want to be clear that this is a certification preparation course.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.840
Every lesson is built against the official exam blueprint. This exam is concept and scenario driven,

5
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.920
and that's exactly how I teach these lessons.

6
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:28.780
This is not a code along, and it's not a project build. If you're looking for a hands-on lab series, this is not it.

7
00:00:29.219 --> 00:00:35.109
What you will get is the mental models and the judgment the exam actually tests in the least amount

8
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:36.120
of time possible.

9
00:00:36.459 --> 00:00:40.860
We didn't fill this course with a bunch of demos and industry examples, and that's on purpose.

10
00:00:41.419 --> 00:00:47.419
Ladies and gentlemen, AI is a race, and we want you to get certified as quickly as possible.

11
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.020
So there's no filler, just what you need for exam day.

12
00:00:52.259 --> 00:00:57.659
Secondly, this course is made for people at varying experience levels. The most common feedback

13
00:00:57.659 --> 00:01:03.099
I hear is that the course either moves too fast or seems repetitive at times. Both are true,

14
00:01:03.099 --> 00:01:06.459
but they're usually about background, not the material itself.

15
00:01:06.739 --> 00:01:13.819
You see, most people watching this have five or more years of professional experience, but from different angles.

16
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.417
Maybe you're a project manager, and the planning sections feel like old news, or maybe you've been

17
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:20.646
writing code for years,

18
00:01:20.699 --> 00:01:23.220
and some of the methodology feels repetitive.

19
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:29.030
I just ask for a little patience with that. Imagine yourself in a room with a big, diverse group of

20
00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.804
people with varying backgrounds.

21
00:01:31.059 --> 00:01:36.500
That's who this course is built for, and we worked hard to make it approachable and useful for all of you.

22
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.819
This course will fill your knowledge gaps and have you prepared for exam day,

23
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.660
but the cost is that you might hear one or two things that you already knew coming in.

24
00:01:46.819 --> 00:01:51.019
A quick note on the slide decks. We do use shorthand phrasing on the slides.

25
00:01:51.059 --> 00:01:56.029
They're meant as a visual anchor. I don't read from the slides either, since they're just an

26
00:01:56.029 --> 00:01:57.325
overview of the content.

27
00:01:57.379 --> 00:02:02.819
That's intentional. The slide anchors the idea, and my narration explains the idea.

28
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:08.484
Focus on what I'm explaining, and let the slide be your visual reference, not a script to read along

29
00:02:08.484 --> 00:02:10.327
with. If you do like reading along,

30
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.779
use the closed caption feature, and you'll get the best of both worlds.

31
00:02:14.539 --> 00:02:17.539
Another one that won't surprise you, I'm not perfect.

32
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:23.860
Sometimes I make a mistake on a slide, or you'll hit a practice question where you're convinced the answer is wrong.

33
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:30.580
Either way, help me and help your fellow students out. Send me a direct message and tell me what you saw.

34
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.706
I'll verify it against the official documentation, and the confirmed fixes will go back into the

35
00:02:35.706 --> 00:02:37.568
course for everyone to benefit from.

36
00:02:38.979 --> 00:02:43.619
Another quick note. Check the resources section of this lecture before you go any further.

37
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.820
We attach a lot of resources, either to this lesson or to the final lesson of the course.

38
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.820
This course ships with multiple downloads, a condensed review of the entire course that we call the study companion,

39
00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.740
a key terms cram sheet for quick reference, a 30-day study plan to structure your prep, and more.

40
00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.976
If you don't see what you're looking for attached to this lesson, check the final lesson of the

41
00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:08.684
course to find those resources.

42
00:03:09.699 --> 00:03:15.100
And one final thing about pace. Some people tell me that I talk too slowly.

43
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:21.139
Others tell me I move too fast. Both are easy to fix using the speed controls in the video player.

44
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.940
Just set the pace where it's comfortable for you.

45
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:31.020
Okay, that's it on this stuff. Let's get started on the actual course content. I'll see you in the first lesson.

